{"text":"An dieser Stelle könnte sich die Frage stellen: Wenn MRT-Befunde so oft nicht aussagekräftig sind – warum macht man dann überhaupt MRTs? Die Antwort ist: Es gibt absolut sinnvolle Indikationen für ein MRT bei Rückenschmerz. Das sind genau die Situationen, die wir in Lektion I.3 als Red Flags besprochen haben. Wenn der Verdacht auf eine ernsthafte Ursache besteht – einen Tumor, eine Entzündung, eine relevante Nervenkompression mit motorischen Ausfällen – dann ist ein MRT das Mittel der Wahl. Wenn es um unspezifischen chronischen Kreuzschmerz geht – also den Schmerz, mit dem wir uns in dieser Masterclass beschäftigen – dann sagen die Leitlinien: Routinemäßiges MRT ist nicht empfohlen. Es bringt selten neue Erkenntnisse und führt häufiger zu mehr Verwirrung als zu mehr Klarheit. Trotzdem werden in der Praxis viele MRTs auch ohne klare Indikation gemacht. Aus Vorsicht, aus Patientenwunsch, aus Routine. Das Ergebnis ist eine Welle von Zufallsbefunden – Veränderungen, die im Bild zu sehen sind, aber keine klinische Relevanz haben. Diese Befunde landen dann im Arztbrief, der Patient liest sie, und der ohnehin schwierige Schmerz bekommt jetzt noch eine schwere medizinische Verpackung. Das ist nicht die Schuld einzelner Ärzte. Das ist ein systemisches Problem moderner Medizin: Wir haben die Bildgebung schneller weiterentwickelt als das klinische Verständnis dafür, was diese Bilder bedeuten.","chars":["A","n"," ","d","i","e","s","e","r"," ","S","t","e","l","l","e"," ","k","ö","n","n","t","e"," ","s","i","c","h"," ","d","i","e"," ","F","r","a","g","e"," ","s","t","e","l","l","e","n",":"," ","W","e","n","n"," ","M","R","T","-","B","e","f","u","n","d","e"," ","s","o"," ","o","f","t"," ","n","i","c","h","t"," ","a","u","s","s","a","g","e","k","r","ä","f","t","i","g"," ","s","i","n","d"," ","–"," ","w","a","r","u","m"," ","m","a","c","h","t"," ","m","a","n"," ","d","a","n","n"," ","ü","b","e","r","h","a","u","p","t"," ","M","R","T","s","?"," ","D","i","e"," ","A","n","t",